WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.757
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.057 --> 00:00:09.489
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:15.484
Unit twelve: Ethnographic Writing. Today you will describe habits in the past with used to and would.

4
00:00:15.784 --> 00:00:18.575
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.847
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:24.742
Field notes.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:29.886
Field notes means written observations made while studying people in their own setting.

8
00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:33.087
Her field notes recorded every conversation.

9
00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:36.626
Your turn. Say: Field notes.

10
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:40.746
Field notes.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:43.239
Participant observation.

12
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.432
Participant observation means joining a group's daily life in order to study it.

13
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:51.655
Participant observation lasted a full year.

14
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.761
Your turn. Say: Participant observation.

15
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.472
Participant observation.

16
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:02.566
Cultural insider.

17
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.256
Cultural insider means a person who belongs to the culture being studied.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.069
A cultural insider may notice what outsiders miss.

19
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:14.928
Your turn. Say: Cultural insider.

20
00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:19.279
Cultural insider.

21
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:21.331
Thick description.

22
00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:27.126
Thick description means a detailed account that explains the meaning of behaviour in context.

23
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:30.482
Thick description explains why the gesture matters.

24
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:33.794
Your turn. Say: Thick description.

25
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:37.901
Thick description.

26
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:39.639
Fieldwork.

27
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.879
Fieldwork means research carried out in real settings, not in a library.

28
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:47.970
Fieldwork took the team to remote villages.

29
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:51.549
Your turn. Say: Fieldwork.

30
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.331
Fieldwork.

31
00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:57.317
Ethnographer.

32
00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:02.650
Ethnographer means a researcher who studies cultures by living among people.

33
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.679
The ethnographer learned the local language.

34
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:09.075
Your turn. Say: Ethnographer.

35
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:13.097
Ethnographer.

36
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.559
Cultural interpretation.

37
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:21.417
Cultural interpretation means an explanation of what customs and actions mean to people.

38
00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:24.492
Cultural interpretation requires humility.

39
00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:28.386
Your turn. Say: Cultural interpretation.

40
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:32.827
Cultural interpretation.

41
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:34.840
Reflexivity.

42
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:40.322
Reflexivity means a writer's awareness of how their own position shapes the research.

43
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:43.671
Reflexivity makes the author's bias visible.

44
00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:47.098
Your turn. Say: Reflexivity.

45
00:02:49.933 --> 00:02:51.271
Reflexivity.

46
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:55.822
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.683 --> 00:03:04.946
Visal, an ethnographer writes 'Every evening the elders would gather and tell stories. They used to sit under the same tree.' What is the difference?

48
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:20.849
Both describe past habits. Would + base verb needs a time context already set, and it is for repeated actions. Used to also describes past states: The village used to be quiet.

49
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.775
Can I use would for a state?

50
00:03:24.883 --> 00:03:35.378
No. Not 'The village would be quiet'. Would is only for repeated actions, not states such as be, have or know. Use used to for states and for actions.

51
00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:38.959
Why is this useful in ethnography?

52
00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:51.877
Field notes often describe routines that no longer exist. Used to and would make that memory vivid without a long list of dates. Do not use either for a single event.

53
00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:54.998
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:00.775
Grammar focus: Past habits: used to and would plus base verb.

55
00:04:01.075 --> 00:04:02.204
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:05.868
Every evening, the elders would gather and tell stories.

57
00:04:06.783 --> 00:04:08.702
They used to sit under the same tree.

58
00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:11.848
The village used to be very quiet.

59
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:23.524
Used to + base verb = past habit or state. Would + base verb = repeated past action only, after a time is set. Never use would for states or single events.

60
00:04:24.283 --> 00:04:27.783
Be careful! Don't say: The river would be wide once.

61
00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:32.406
Say: The river used to be wide.

62
00:04:32.706 --> 00:04:35.298
Use used to for past states, not would.

63
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.764
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:40.997
Notes from the Village.

65
00:04:41.297 --> 00:04:45.445
An ethnographer spends months in fieldwork, writing field notes each night.

66
00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:50.883
Through participant observation, she learned that the elders would meet at dawn and share news.

67
00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:55.677
A cultural insider told her that the market used to be much larger.

68
00:04:56.667 --> 00:05:01.337
Thick description records not only what people do but also what their actions mean to them.

69
00:05:02.333 --> 00:05:07.096
Reflexivity reminds her that her own background shapes every cultural interpretation.

70
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.480
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.317 --> 00:05:15.397
The elders blank meet every morning.

72
00:05:15.697 --> 00:05:18.538
A, would. B, were. C, have.

73
00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.786
The answer is A: would.

74
00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.459
Joining a group's daily life to study it is participant blank.

75
00:05:28.759 --> 00:05:33.353
A, observation. B, notes. C, reflexivity.

76
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.097
The answer is A: observation.

77
00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:41.832
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:42.132 --> 00:05:48.328
A, The river would be wider once.. B, The river used to be wider.. C, The river use to be wider..

79
00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:54.934
The answer is B: The river used to be wider..

80
00:05:56.083 --> 00:06:00.022
A detailed account that explains the meaning of behaviour is thick blank.

81
00:06:00.322 --> 00:06:04.114
A, data. B, fieldwork. C, description.

82
00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:10.015
The answer is C: description.

83
00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.856
According to the reading, what does reflexivity remind the writer about?

84
00:06:15.156 --> 00:06:19.111
A, her own background. B, the weather. C, the budget.

85
00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:25.049
The answer is A: her own background.

86
00:06:26.233 --> 00:06:29.536
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:30.383 --> 00:06:35.355
Why might an ethnographer need to reflect on their own biases while writing about another culture?

88
00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.465
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.765 --> 00:07:11.361
An ethnographer is always a person with a background, language and beliefs, and these can shape what is noticed and how it is explained. If the writer does not reflect, they may present their own assumptions as facts about another culture. Reflexivity makes these influences visible, so the reader can judge the account fairly and the community is described with respect.

90
00:07:11.661 --> 00:07:13.475
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.383 --> 00:07:20.693
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past habits: used to and would plus base verb.

92
00:07:20.993 --> 00:07:26.362
Today's value is reflective awareness. Notice your own experience with care and honesty.

93
00:07:26.662 --> 00:07:29.500
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.940
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

